  "theory": {
    "intro": "Any polygon can be split into triangles by drawing all diagonals from one vertex. A polygon with $n$ sides splits into $n-2$ triangles, and each triangle contributes $180^{\\circ}$, so the interior angles add to $180(n-2)$ degrees.",
    "facts": [
      "The interior angle sum of a polygon with $n$ sides is $S=180(n-2)$ degrees.",
      "Drawing every diagonal from one vertex of an $n$-gon makes $n-2$ triangles; a quadrilateral gives $2$, a pentagon $3$, a hexagon $4$, and so on.",
      "To find a missing interior angle, first work out the sum $S$, then set the angles equal to $S$ and solve.",
      "The rule works for any polygon, not just regular ones — the angles need not be equal.",
      "To find the number of sides from a known angle sum, solve $180(n-2)=S$ for $n$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Find the angle sum using $S=180(n-2)$.",
      "Form an equation from the angles.",
      "Solve for the unknown."
    ]
  },
